the eu's strategic priorities  in the last years  we have made significant steps  in stepping up europe's defense  cooperation  but the magnitude of the events we are  facing  represents a watershed moment for europe  as general bulkheart the french chief of  staff of the french army explained a few  days ago  for 20 years we have dealt mostly with  counter-terrorism operations  we are now seeing a conflict on a truly  different scale  end of quote  and he's so right  the military threat to europe has  fundamentally changed  all member states will have to review  their national force structure and  posture  and it is already clear  that we are collectively facing a  massive investment gap  defending europe from russian military  aggression requires a different level of  cooperation  from innovation and development to  procurement and operational use  first we need to collectively identify  the capabilities  that are required to defend europe in  this new strategic reality  second at a deeper level  we have to have another conversation  about the type of defense industrial  base we need in europe as a result of  the russian aggression  the european defense fund  can help to incentivize spending on  common defense projects  and to strengthen the defense industrial  base in europe  but it needs to be strengthened itself  and thirdly  it is important  to identify and close the most pressing  capability and investment gaps in this  sector  systems like air and missile defense  or enhance cyber security  and defense  all those require different level of  cooperation  thus  i will present by may an analysis of our  defense investment needs gaps  and opportunities  both at national  and at european level  it will be crucial that our resources  are invested strategically on joint  european projects  that ensure interoperability in the  field  and enable our defence industry to scale  up across national borders  we need to spend not only more  but more together  it is time for our european defense  union to enter the stage  honorable members this brings me to my  second point  beyond military means europe will only  be secure if we end our over-reliance on  russian gas  we simply cannot rely anymore on a  supplier  that uses energy to put pressure on us  we have to diversify our supply away  from russia and to reliable suppliers  and we have to massively invest in  renewable energy  and the good news is  we have everything in place to act  with the european green deal  the european union is the world leader  in the green transformation  the direction of travel is set  and now we will accelerate  the green transition is a strategic  investment in our independence and in  our security  and this is why last week the european  commission unveiled re-power eu  we will reduce eu demand for russian gas  first by fast tracking new  renewable energy projects including  hydrogen and biogas  second by speeding up investment in  energy efficiency  to roll out  for example solar panels wind and heat  pumps  those are low hanging fruits  as is the insulation of buildings  and third we are diversifying our gas  supplies  the united states norway azerbaijan and  others are stepping up delivery  finally  the investment in lng infrastructure  pays off  because we can use it increasingly for  hydrogen  thus we can end our over-reliance on  russian  gas well before 2030.  we just discussed this intensively at  leaders level in versailles  it is doable and we will go for it  we also discussed in versailles last  week how to meet the momentous  responsibilities we face  and the higher levels of investments  over time  while maintaining fiscal sustainability  investments must be strategic  to strengthen our capacities in defense  cybersecurity energy  critical supply chains semiconductors  health  and food security  and we need to mobilize the private  sector  national parliaments should play a  central role in this conversation  you are the elected representatives of  european citizens  your scrutiny and your commitment are  essential  to turn european investment into local  growth  and to shape the reforms each of your  countries need  your voice will be essential in the  debate on our future economic governance  we need an open debate  and our aim is  to achieve a consensus by the end of the  year  honourable members  the events of these weeks are an  unprecedented test for our union  we have acted with speed and resolve  and we will continue to rise to the  challenge we are facing  together  because unity is our best strength  the coming weeks and months will be  difficult  but together  we can help our ukrainian friends in the  toughest of times  together we can make this war  unsustainable for the kremlin  and give diplomacy a chance  this is what our union stands for  so  long live democracy  long live ukraine  and long live europe
 

